           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/powershell/" title="View all posts in Powershell" rel="category tag">Powershell</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2007/06/powershell-profile-on-roaming-profile/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on PowerShell Profile on Roaming Profile"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="683 /blogs/ryanc/archive/2007/06/27/powershell-profile-on-roaming-profile.aspx">View Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>NOTE:&nbsp;Impatience&nbsp;just leads to&nbsp;frustration. When I first starting playing with PowerShell here at the office, I was all excited to start adding little cmdlets to my personal profile and add to my stellar productivity (tongue firmly planted in check).&nbsp; This would hopefully reduce the net loss incurred by my learning PowerShell in the first place.&nbsp; So [...]</p>

